WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:06.690
spaced repetition systems, SRS, Anki.
Most language learning systems, including

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:12.780
LingQ, do incorporate some form of spaced
repetition into their program of learning.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.100
It's a tool to enable people
to remember words primarily.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.330
Although SRSs can be used in
science, can be used in other fields.

00:00:21.495 --> 00:00:26.175
Even in language, it can be used
to try to remember phrases and words.

00:00:26.235 --> 00:00:31.064
The whole idea is that with a system of
spaced repetition, and specifically an

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:36.765
expanding space, in other words, there is
an algorithm which someone or different

00:00:36.765 --> 00:00:41.325
people have different algorithms, which
say that if you space the repetition out

00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:45.885
initially, let's say two days, 10 days,
30 days, 50 days, whatever it might be,

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:50.295
that this is going to improve your ability
to retain the items that you're learning.

00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:56.250
So, I have not been a great practitioner
of spaced repetition systems or a user

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.050
of these spaced repetition systems.

00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:02.279
I did, however, use a form of
space repetition when I was

00:01:02.279 --> 00:01:05.880
learning Chinese characters a
long, long time ago, 50 years ago.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:06.380
I

00:01:06.539 --> 00:01:13.110
had a box here of these lovely
hand drawn Chinese characters, and

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.310
there's a thousand of them here, and I
went through all one thousand characters

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.759
with these squared Chinese textbooks.

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:24.810
And I would write one character,
let's say 10, 15 times,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.400
and then a couple of columns over.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.759
I would put either the sound, the
pronunciation, or the meaning.

00:01:29.785 --> 00:01:33.505
Then I'd pick up another character
or another flashcard, and I would

00:01:33.505 --> 00:01:35.664
continue doing that, write it 10 times.

00:01:35.664 --> 00:01:39.384
Eventually I ran into the first
card and I'd have to remember

00:01:39.384 --> 00:01:42.175
it before I had forgotten it,
and I would keep on doing that.

00:01:42.414 --> 00:01:43.735
I started out with 10 a day.

00:01:43.735 --> 00:01:45.085
I got it up to 30 a day.

00:01:45.085 --> 00:01:45.824
Of course,

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.590
forgetting a lot, relearning.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.850
And I did that for a thousand characters.
Thereafter, to learn the remaining

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.740
3,000 characters that I had to learn,

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:58.030
I just relied on coming across them in
my reading, writing them out a few times.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.160
So that's my experience
with space repetition.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.380
Of course, that was not an algorithm.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.160
It was not an expanding space,
spaced repetition system.

00:02:06.345 --> 00:02:09.164
It was just some form of spacing.

00:02:09.285 --> 00:02:12.614
Now, doing some research on the subject,
I discovered that while there are

00:02:12.614 --> 00:02:17.174
studies that show that some kind
of expanding spaced repetition is

00:02:17.174 --> 00:02:20.565
beneficial to learning, there are
other studies that show that it is the

00:02:20.565 --> 00:02:22.484
repetition that's beneficial to learning.

00:02:22.785 --> 00:02:26.654
That even if you have constant spacing,
there is no significant difference.

00:02:26.654 --> 00:02:30.674
And I will leave some links here in the,
uh, description box so that you can go

00:02:30.674 --> 00:02:32.234
and check these different people out.

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:36.464
So it's not obvious that all of
the research confirms that what we

00:02:36.464 --> 00:02:40.185
consider to be space repetition,
this expanding spacing is all

00:02:40.185 --> 00:02:41.475
that beneficial for learning.

00:02:41.565 --> 00:02:45.494
Now the next thing is applying
this to language learning. Because

00:02:45.554 --> 00:02:46.755
it's one thing if we're learning,

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:51.370
you know, facts, scientific facts, even
historical data. Or even in the case of my

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.110
Chinese characters, while these characters
were being learned sort of away from any

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:00.040
context, at least, there is a significant
connection between the characters

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.150
because there's a lot of repetition
of elements in these characters.

00:03:04.155 --> 00:03:08.350
So that I was learning and getting
a greater and a better sense of how

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.950
these different elements combined
in these characters that I was

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.450
learning when we're learning words.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.090
There still are some
elements or components.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.950
But it's not as clear as was the case
when I was learning Chinese characters.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.970
So when we learn a language, of course
we're concerned about input, we're

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.200
concerned about comprehension, and we are
concerned about producing the language.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.340
And so we need, obviously, we need
words in order to understand the

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.050
language and eventually to produce it.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.890
And as you know, I'm a big
proponent of inputb-based learning,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.270
getting the language in you.

00:03:39.315 --> 00:03:42.195
Getting words in you so that
you understand the language

00:03:42.195 --> 00:03:43.335
and eventually can use it.

00:03:43.454 --> 00:03:48.795
So in studying how the brain learns, there
is a thing called "Event-Related Potential,"

00:03:48.945 --> 00:03:53.204
which measures how the brain deals
with sentences that it hears or reads.

00:03:53.295 --> 00:03:59.085
So the brain tends to predict what's
going to happen in this sentence or

00:03:59.144 --> 00:04:01.905
phrase or whatever. And it basically has

00:04:02.305 --> 00:04:07.075
certain predictions in terms of the
meanings, sort of the semantics if

00:04:07.075 --> 00:04:09.174
you want, and in terms of the syntax.

00:04:09.174 --> 00:04:14.125
In other words, how the structure should
work based on its familiarity with the

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.135
language, its exposure to the language.

00:04:16.135 --> 00:04:18.355
It makes certain assumptions.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:23.804
And in this way, researchers are able to
tell what is important for the brain

00:04:23.804 --> 00:04:25.934
to be able to understand that sentence.

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:30.494
Well, it turns out that while frequency
of exposure is extremely important,

00:04:30.585 --> 00:04:33.645
lexical frequency, in other words,
how often they've seen the word.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:39.550
However, as the learner has more and
more exposure to a variety of contexts,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:44.140
the lexical frequency, in other words,
frequency as a factor becomes much

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:49.330
less important than previous exposure
to a variety of different contexts.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:54.430
And in the description, they talk
about the lexical frequency being

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.710
sort of a bottoms up, bits and pieces

00:04:56.895 --> 00:05:01.304
approach to how the learner reacts
to words and phrases in a sentence.

00:05:01.335 --> 00:05:04.995
Whereas over time, with enough
exposure to the language, it's more

00:05:04.995 --> 00:05:10.455
of a top down form of reaction that
dominates our ability to understand

00:05:10.455 --> 00:05:14.895
and eventually to predict what's going to
happen in the sense or to react if

00:05:14.955 --> 00:05:18.645
what happened in the sentence didn't
correspond to what our brain expected.

00:05:18.895 --> 00:05:23.335
But it's the buildup of that familiarity
with the context, what to expect,

00:05:23.335 --> 00:05:27.775
which is more important than the
frequency of exposure to the words.

00:05:27.865 --> 00:05:28.975
So that is important.

00:05:29.065 --> 00:05:32.185
Bear in mind that, as I said when
I was talking about Spitzer's

00:05:32.185 --> 00:05:36.205
book, learning takes place when
we have frequency of exposure.

00:05:36.265 --> 00:05:40.285
When there is similarity. Obviously,
if I'm going to study, say on LingQ,

00:05:40.345 --> 00:05:41.875
Finish, as opposed to Dutch,

00:05:42.195 --> 00:05:45.405
I'm going to have an easier time with Dutch
because it's more similar to what I know.

00:05:45.525 --> 00:05:49.815
Also in that similarity, I think
there's also included in that

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:51.525
some sense of association with

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.849
similar things, related things.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.230
All of this contributes to whether or
not we're going to get the neurons firing,

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.090
whether the synapse is going to take place.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.719
But the other thing, the third thing,
was relevance or importance, which

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:07.380
is also an important consideration
in determining whether the brain is

00:06:07.385 --> 00:06:08.730
going to choose to learn this thing.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:09.225
If

00:06:09.225 --> 00:06:11.205
it considers it to be,
you know, important.

00:06:11.295 --> 00:06:14.385
It's important to bear these things
in mind when we consider how a

00:06:14.385 --> 00:06:16.665
space repetition system works.

00:06:16.725 --> 00:06:21.945
Obviously, if we take the position that
exposure to a lot of content and a lot

00:06:21.945 --> 00:06:27.015
of context reduces the importance of the
frequency of exposure to the word itself,

00:06:27.045 --> 00:06:28.125
that's a consideration.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:34.530
And if our approach to comprehension
is very much tied up with context

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:39.090
and relating words to other words
in context that we have seen before,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.620
structures that we have seen before,
it reduces the specific importance

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.860
of just the frequency of exposure to
the word, which is kind of what spaced

00:06:46.865 --> 00:06:48.960
repetition system tends to stress.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:49.979
That's insofar as

00:06:50.145 --> 00:06:51.465
comprehension is concerned.

00:06:51.495 --> 00:06:52.844
Now let's look at production.

00:06:52.875 --> 00:06:56.775
The interesting thing about production
is that planning and memory are

00:06:56.775 --> 00:06:58.455
located in the same parts of our brain.

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:02.145
Because to plan something, you have
to be able to remember something that

00:07:02.145 --> 00:07:03.825
you want to plan to do in the future.

00:07:03.854 --> 00:07:07.424
And when we produce a language,
apparently, and I, again, I'll leave

00:07:07.424 --> 00:07:10.484
a link to this in the description box,
there's a lot of planning involved.

00:07:10.484 --> 00:07:11.625
What are we going to say?

00:07:11.625 --> 00:07:12.914
How are we going to structure it?

00:07:12.914 --> 00:07:14.234
Which words are we going to use?

00:07:14.294 --> 00:07:16.305
And the planning involves a process of,

00:07:16.325 --> 00:07:20.645
first of all, using the
easily retrieved words first.

00:07:20.705 --> 00:07:21.815
Because that gets you going.

00:07:21.935 --> 00:07:27.755
Reusing patterns that have worked for you
before and suppressing those things that

00:07:27.784 --> 00:07:30.395
might seem similar but aren't relevant.

00:07:30.545 --> 00:07:34.594
A word that sounds almost the same
or is spelled almost the same. Or

00:07:34.594 --> 00:07:36.034
interference from another language.

00:07:36.145 --> 00:07:37.235
We have to suppress those.

00:07:37.235 --> 00:07:41.405
So the brain has sort of a three-part
process, finding the easily

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:45.094
retrieved words and phrases to use,
reusing things that worked before.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.240
And suppressing those
things that they don't want.

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.810
But all of this requires
a significant memory.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:54.800
And if that memory is best achieved
through massive exposure to the

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.360
language rather than isolated
review of words, maybe that's

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:00.980
where we should orient ourselves.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:04.580
If we look at spaced repetition systems
per se, it seems that in the case

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.940
of language, language learners might
have, depending on where they are in

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:10.130
the language, 500 words in their deck.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:13.875
5,000 words in their deck. A large number
of words potentially in their deck.

00:08:13.935 --> 00:08:17.835
Now, language learning is a function of
attitude, of time, and of the ability

00:08:17.835 --> 00:08:19.335
to focus on what it is you're doing.

00:08:19.335 --> 00:08:23.235
So obviously, if you like doing
spaced repetition, if you like doing

00:08:23.235 --> 00:08:24.975
flashcards, it's something you should do.

00:08:25.035 --> 00:08:28.606
The research that I did (I went to
ChatGPT) suggested that the average

00:08:28.611 --> 00:08:30.885
person spends 30 seconds on a flashcard.

00:08:31.085 --> 00:08:33.575
I can't imagine spending
30 seconds on a flashcard.

00:08:33.635 --> 00:08:36.635
I do, and I'll explain a little
later how I use flashcards, but

00:08:36.815 --> 00:08:38.674
in my case, it's a few seconds.

00:08:38.674 --> 00:08:39.845
It's not 30 seconds.

00:08:39.875 --> 00:08:40.895
It's just exposure.

00:08:40.895 --> 00:08:43.804
But I guess people who are trying
to remember that flashcard might

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:45.515
spend 30 seconds on a flashcard.

00:08:45.575 --> 00:08:48.155
At least that's what ChatGPT told me.

00:08:48.215 --> 00:08:52.775
So if we look at word frequency,
as I've said before, word frequency

00:08:52.805 --> 00:08:54.605
declines very, very quickly.

00:08:54.635 --> 00:08:59.615
So in the most frequent words,
obviously just in any content,

00:08:59.675 --> 00:09:03.935
any context, you are going to get
exposed to the very frequent words.

00:09:03.965 --> 00:09:07.505
The bigger issue is if we look at word
frequency, and I'll show you a list

00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:11.705
here and you'll see that words that
are in the 50th spot in terms of word

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:19.530
Frequency, or 500th spot or 5000th spot,
may only appear once or twice in a book.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:24.420
In other words, these statistics
were based on Google's corpora of

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:28.530
content, but some of these words,
even fairly common words, like, you

00:09:28.535 --> 00:09:32.040
know, "road" or I don't know, whatever,
they may not appear that often.

00:09:32.255 --> 00:09:33.245
How are you going to learn them?

00:09:33.305 --> 00:09:35.555
Obviously, you can guide the process.

00:09:35.584 --> 00:09:38.765
If you're reading "Harry Potter" the word
"Harry" is going to appear a lot of times.

00:09:38.765 --> 00:09:42.064
I've never read "Harry Potter" but words
having to do with magicians and so

00:09:42.064 --> 00:09:43.594
forth are going to be there more often.

00:09:43.625 --> 00:09:47.405
If you're talking about economics or
history, there are certain relatively

00:09:47.435 --> 00:09:50.974
rare words that will appear more
often. But nevertheless, you are

00:09:50.974 --> 00:09:56.675
chasing a very small number or 
a low frequency of occurrence.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:00.510
Of these words that you nevertheless
require, because on any given page,

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:05.220
there's going to be 10 of these low-frequency
words. And not understanding

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:08.970
them is going to limit your ability
to understand what's on the page.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.290
So then perhaps that's a place
to use spaced repetition systems,

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:15.300
because you can then go after words

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.090
that won't appear naturally often
enough for you to learn them.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.540
But with spaced repetition,
you can then zoom in on them.

00:10:21.745 --> 00:10:26.515
The problem is, and this is the dilemma,
if there are potentially 10-, 20,000 words

00:10:26.515 --> 00:10:31.135
that you need to learn, if you actually
do them in spaced repetition systems,

00:10:31.165 --> 00:10:35.755
you will end up spending almost all
your time on these flashcard reviews.

00:10:35.785 --> 00:10:38.605
Obviously, if you have seven or eight
hours a day to spend learning the

00:10:38.605 --> 00:10:42.565
language, then that might be where you
choose to spend your time. And let me

00:10:42.565 --> 00:10:46.735
stress, it's entirely a matter of personal
choice. So I would not choose to do that.

00:10:46.765 --> 00:10:48.115
In fact, I don't choose to do that.

00:10:48.115 --> 00:10:50.215
But people who enjoy doing that, then

00:10:50.495 --> 00:10:51.485
that's what they should do.

00:10:51.665 --> 00:10:56.435
I find that when I'm reviewing
flashcards, if it's divorced from any

00:10:56.435 --> 00:10:58.925
context, then I quickly lose interest.

00:10:58.955 --> 00:11:00.425
So I find it hard to focus.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:04.205
So once you're not focusing, once
you no longer consider it relevant,

00:11:04.205 --> 00:11:06.035
important, then I can't continue.

00:11:06.095 --> 00:11:09.635
So I occasionally do go through
lists at LingQ, especially of

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.210
my Status 3 words.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.220
Trying to move them to
known because I have a goal.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:17.210
I want to increase my Known Words total,
but still I can only do it for so long.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:18.890
10, 15 minutes max.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.760
It's all about sort of "bits and pieces
learning" versus "top down learning."

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:26.450
When we're dealing with flashcards,
when we're reviewing grammar rules, we

00:11:26.455 --> 00:11:27.920
are dealing with the bits and pieces.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.470
When we are just reading and
listening, we're in the sort of

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:34.430
"top down learning mode," and I
prefer to spend most of my time

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:37.960
in a "top down learning mode."
But there is sort of a compromise,

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:39.910
which I now really enjoy doing.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:44.950
In the case of my Persian, given where
I am, call it a [CEFR] B1 level in Persian,

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:48.370
still struggling with a writing system
that's still not very familiar to me.

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.130
I enjoy going into Sentence Review.

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.020
The content that I'm using is timestamped.

00:11:53.285 --> 00:11:54.245
I can listen to it.

00:11:54.275 --> 00:11:57.755
I can go through it, look at the
words that are still not known to me.

00:11:57.815 --> 00:12:01.895
I'm still dealing with content that I've
read before, so there are no more sort

00:12:01.895 --> 00:12:05.855
of unknown words, but there's a lot of
yellow words that I still don't yet know.

00:12:05.915 --> 00:12:10.445
If I want to, I can go into any one
of these sentences and go through the

00:12:10.445 --> 00:12:13.205
matching pairs flashcards exercise.

00:12:13.265 --> 00:12:16.655
I can go through the exercise
of reconstituting the sentence.

00:12:16.885 --> 00:12:20.425
That's really sort of drilling down
into "bits and pieces learning,"

00:12:20.484 --> 00:12:22.194
which, of course, slows me down.

00:12:22.224 --> 00:12:23.574
So I don't do a lot of it.

00:12:23.844 --> 00:12:28.074
I do it on a sampling basis because I
think it helps give me a better sense

00:12:28.074 --> 00:12:29.604
of the structure of the language.

00:12:29.635 --> 00:12:33.444
It just increases, at some level, my
familiarity with the language, even

00:12:33.449 --> 00:12:37.675
though it's slows down my taking in
as much of the language as possible.

00:12:37.704 --> 00:12:41.035
And I think the lesson here, or the
message that I would like to end

00:12:41.035 --> 00:12:43.885
with, is in language learning, of
course, our attitude is important.

00:12:43.955 --> 00:12:47.705
The amount of time we spend, and remaining
focused so that we pay attention to

00:12:47.705 --> 00:12:48.755
what's happening in the language.

00:12:48.785 --> 00:12:51.335
So therefore, we need to
do what we like doing.

00:12:51.365 --> 00:12:54.635
We need to do what we're going to
happily spend time doing, and we

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:58.115
need to do things that we think
are important so we remain focused.

00:12:58.115 --> 00:13:01.265
And so I don't think any
one system, you know, you should

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:02.675
do this or you should do that.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:05.135
You should use SRS,
should not use SRS.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.189
I don't think that's good advice.

00:13:07.219 --> 00:13:10.550
We never really know when we're
engaged in some language learning

00:13:10.579 --> 00:13:14.750
activity, how effective that activity
is compared to some other activity.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:19.280
We know that as long as we are engaged
with the language in some way, we are

00:13:19.285 --> 00:13:23.540
learning. Now, are we learning less well
than if we were doing something else?

00:13:23.689 --> 00:13:26.479
Are we learning faster than if
we were doing something else?

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.900
It's very hard to tell if we have
a sense that we're improving.

